---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { KEYS, getDiatonicChords } from "../lib/circleOfFifths.ts";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;

// Built from the same function the home page's live table uses, so this
// worked example can never drift out of step with what the app shows.
const cMajorChords = getDiatonicChords(KEYS[0], "major");
---

<Layout title="Chords — Chromesthesia Circle of Fifths">
  <main>
    <h1>Chords, numerals and functions</h1>

    <section class="theory-section">
      <h2>Building a triad</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>triad</strong> is a
        three-note chord: pick a note of the scale, then add the note two steps
        up and the note four steps up — every other note. Whether the result
        sounds major, minor or diminished depends only on how many semitones
        those two additions land at:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Major</strong> — 4 semitones to the middle note, 7 to the top.</li>
        <li><strong>Minor</strong> — 3 semitones to the middle note, 7 to the top.</li>
        <li><strong>Diminished</strong> — 3 semitones to the middle note, 6 to the top.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That single semitone difference in the middle note is the whole
        difference between a chord sounding bright and sounding sad.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="theory-section">
      <h2>The seven chords of a key</h2>
      <p>
        Build a triad on each of the seven notes of a scale, using only notes
        from that scale, and you get the seven chords that belong to the key —
        its <em>diatonic</em> chords. Their pattern of qualities is the same in
        every major key, which is why the wheel can label them at all. Each
        degree also has a traditional name for the job it does, and a
        <strong>roman numeral</strong>
        that encodes its quality: uppercase for major, lowercase for minor, and
        a small ° for diminished.
      </p>
      <table class="chord-table">
        <caption>The seven diatonic chords of C major</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Chord</th>
            <th scope="col">Function</th>
            <th scope="col">Root</th>
            <th scope="col">Quality</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            cMajorChords.map((chord) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{chord.numeral}</td>
                <td>{chord.functionName}</td>
                <td>{chord.root}</td>
                <td>{chord.quality}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The two that do most of the work are the <strong>dominant</strong> (V)
        and the <strong>subdominant</strong> (IV): V tends to pull back to the
        tonic, which is why so much music ends V → I. In a minor key the same
        construction gives a different pattern — i, ii°, III, iv, v, VI, VII —
        because the natural minor scale's own notes differ, and its seventh
        degree sits a whole step below the tonic rather than a half step, so
        it's called the <em>subtonic</em> instead of the leading tone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="theory-section">
      <h2>Why only three slices light up</h2>
      <p>
        Pick a key on the home page and roman numerals appear on six wedges,
        spread across just three slices of the wheel — the key itself, its
        dominant, and its subdominant. That isn't a simplification; it's the
        reason the circle of fifths is useful. Six of a key's seven chords are
        the major and minor chords of exactly those three neighbouring
        positions: for C major, I and vi sit in C's own slice, V and iii in G's,
        and IV and ii in F's.
      </p>
      <p>
        The seventh, vii°, is diminished. It has no key of its own on the wheel,
        so it gets no wedge — but it still appears in the chord table, which
        lists all seven.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="theory-section">
      <h2>Hearing it</h2>
      <p>
        The keyboard's white keys are labelled with an octave number —
        <code>C5</code>, <code>D5</code> and so on. That's
        <strong>scientific pitch notation</strong>:
        the letter names the note, the number says which octave it belongs to,
        and the number ticks over at every C rather than at every A. It's how
        two keys that are both "C" can be told apart.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pitch each key actually plays comes from
        <strong>12-tone equal temperament</strong>
        tuned to A440: the octave is divided into twelve equal steps, so each
        semitone multiplies the frequency by the twelfth root of two (about
        1.0595), counting up or down from A4 at 440 Hz. Every note you hear here
        is worked out with that one formula.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Arpeggio / Chord</strong> toggle changes only how a chord is
        delivered, not what it contains: arpeggio plays the three notes one after
        another, chord sounds them together. And because every chord is described
        as a scale degree rather than as fixed notes, the same tune can be
        rebuilt in any key —
        <strong>transposition</strong>.
        That's what the Happy Birthday button does: it plays the tune's shape,
        starting from whichever key you picked.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which colour each of those keys is drawn in is a separate story — see the
        <a href={`${base}colour.html`}>Colour</a> page.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
